  "Number": 7,
  "Question": "A straight line joins the points \\((-1,1)\\) and \\((3,9).\\) Its \\(x\\)-intercept is:",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(-\\dfrac32\\)",
    "B": "\\(-\\dfrac23\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\dfrac25\\)",
    "D": "\\(2\\)",
    "E": "\\(3\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "A",
  "Solution": "Along a nonvertical straight line, the ratio of vertical change to horizontal change is constant. The given points have different \\(x\\)-coordinates, so the slope \\(m\\) is\n\\[\nm=\\frac{9-1}{3-(-1)}=\\frac84=2.\n\\]\nAt the \\(x\\)-intercept, \\(y=0\\). Starting from \\((-1,1)\\), reaching \\(y=0\\) requires a fall of \\(1\\) in \\(y\\). Because the slope is \\(2\\), this corresponds to a fall of \\(1/2\\) in \\(x\\). Therefore the \\(x\\)-intercept is\n\\[\nx=-1-\\frac12=-\\frac32,\n\\]\nwhich is choice A.",
